---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { company, photo } from "../data/company";
import QuoteForm from "../components/QuoteForm.astro";
import CallStrip from "../components/CallStrip.astro";
const lead = photo("chimney-repairs-bromley-brick-stack");
---

<Layout
  title="Chimney & Guttering Work in Bromley | LeonEast Roofing"
  description="Chimney repairs and guttering across Bromley. Lead flashings, repointing, flaunching, rebuilds and removals, plus gutters, fascias and soffits cleared or replaced."
  lead={lead}
>
  <section class="bg-carbon text-white">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-12 grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-10 items-center">
      <div>
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-volt-bright">Chimneys &amp; guttering</p>
        <h1 class="mt-2 text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold leading-tight">Chimneys and guttering</h1>
        <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-mist">
          The two least interesting parts of a roof and between them the cause of a
          remarkable amount of internal damp. Both are cheap to sort early and expensive to
          sort late.
        </p>
        <a href={company.phoneLink} class="mt-7 inline-flex rounded bg-volt px-6 py-3 text-lg font-bold text-white hover:bg-volt-deep">
          {company.phone}
        </a>
      </div>
      <img src={lead} alt="A brick chimney stack rising above a tiled roof"
        class="w-full rounded-lg object-cover aspect-[4/3]" width="1200" height="900"
        fetchpriority="high" loading="eager" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-14 grid lg:grid-cols-3 gap-10">
    <div class="lg:col-span-2">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-carbon">Chimneys</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-graphite/80">
        If we had to name the most misdiagnosed damp in a house it would be the chimney, and
        it is not close. A stack is exposed on all four sides, has no roof of its own, and is
        held together by mortar that is often a century old. Water gets in at the top through
        cracked flaunching or open joints, runs down inside the flue, and shows up on a wall
        a floor or two below - which is why people spend money on entirely the wrong wall.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-graphite/80">
        The other thing we find constantly is a cement fillet where the lead flashing should
        be. A lot of stacks had theirs replaced with a triangle of sand and cement at some
        point in the eighties, because it is quicker. Cement is rigid, the stack and the roof
        move independently, and it always cracks in the end. Lead flexes and lasts forty to
        sixty years.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-graphite/80">
        Worth knowing: not all chimney damp is a leak. It can be condensation in a cold
        unventilated flue, or salts drawn out of an old lining. Three different problems,
        three different fixes, so we look inside as well as up before recommending anything.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-graphite/80">
        Flashings in lead are £450 to £950. Repointing above the roof line is £600 to £1,500 -
        and on older soft brickwork it wants a lime mix, because hard modern cement traps
        moisture and pushes the decay into the bricks. New flaunching with the pots rebedded
        is £350 to £700, much less if it is done while the access is already up. Taking a
        redundant stack down to below the roof line and making good is £900 to £2,000.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-carbon">Guttering, fascias and soffits</h2>
      <img src={photo("blocked-gutter-leaves-autumn-roof")}
        alt="A roof gutter filled with fallen autumn leaves"
        class="mt-5 w-full rounded-lg object-cover aspect-[3/2]" width="800" height="533" loading="lazy" />
      <p class="mt-6 text-graphite/80">
        Clearing a gutter is the cheapest job on this website and it prevents the most
        expensive one. A gutter that overflows at one corner soaks the fascia behind it, the
        fascia rots, the rafter ends follow, and a fifty pound bracket job becomes several
        thousand pounds of timber work. Bromley is one of the greenest boroughs in London,
        which is lovely to live in and hard on gutters every October.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-graphite/80">
        When a fascia does need replacing we take the old board off rather than capping over
        it. Capping means fixing new plastic over the existing timber - quicker, cheaper, and
        it seals a rotten board and whatever is happening to the rafter ends behind a nice
        new cover. You cannot tell the difference once it is finished, which is exactly why
        it is worth asking about.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-graphite/80">
        Clearing gutters and downpipes on a standard semi is £90 to £180. New guttering is
        £25 to £45 a metre. A full roofline - gutters, fascias and soffits together - runs
        £1,400 to £3,200 depending on the length and the access.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-carbon">A few questions we get</h2>
      <dl class="mt-5 space-y-5">
        <div>
          <dt class="font-bold text-carbon">My chimney breast is damp but the roof looks fine.</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-graphite/80">That is the classic. It usually means water is getting in at the top of the stack rather than through the roof at all. Worth having looked at before winter.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="font-bold text-carbon">Can you take a chimney down?</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-graphite/80">Yes, normally to below the roof line with the covering made good over the top. Removing the breast inside the house too is a bigger and structural job. We will be honest about whether you will regret losing the fireplace.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="font-bold text-carbon">How often should gutters be cleared?</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-graphite/80">Once a year, late autumn, after the leaves are down. Twice if you have mature trees directly overhead, which on a lot of streets round here you do.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="font-bold text-carbon">There are birds nesting in my soffit.</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-graphite/80">Then it has to wait. Nesting birds are legally protected while the nest is in use. We will make a temporary repair to keep the weather out and come back properly from late summer.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="lg:sticky lg:top-6 self-start">
      <QuoteForm title="Get a price" source="Chimneys and guttering" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CallStrip heading="Gutters worth doing before winter?"
    text="It is the cheapest thing we do and it heads off the most expensive. Ring and we will book you in." />
</Layout>
